**Theorem 27.2 (singleton upper with explicit actual acquisition).**
For every $D\ge1$ and every $p\in\mathcal P_D$ of length $n$,

$$
M_D(\{p\})\le m(n).
\tag{GDB.110}
$$

There is a deterministic source-blind preparation attaining exactly
$m(n)$ complete names, with exactly $n$ original-root Reads, maximum
address length $n$ and exactly $n(n+1)/2$ submitted letters on every
source. Its late reader uses one original-root prefix Read of length
at most $n$, before every supplied count. The construction serves the
whole variable-shape $\mathcal F_D$.

Proof. First $0\le r\le m-1$, by the two parity cases in (GDB.104).
Consequently $0\le a\le\lfloor m/2\rfloor$ and
$0\le b\le\lfloor(m-1)/2\rfloor$: if $r\le\lfloor m/2\rfloor$
then $b=0$; otherwise
$b=r-\lfloor m/2\rfloor\le m-1-\lfloor m/2\rfloor$.
Thus $2a\le m$ and $2b+1\le m$. These inequalities verify every
range and injectivity assertion in Definition 27.1, including $n=1$.
There are $a$ earlier positions, $m$ centers and $b$ later positions.

Preparation submits $p_1,\ldots,p_n$ in that fixed public order,
even after a leaf has been seen. Such absent Reads remain legal and
charged. It computes $\partial_p(t)$ from these replies, then exports
only $i=g_n(\partial_p(t))$. Every $i\in[m]$ is attained by either
actual center-leaf witness from Lemma 26.3. Hence the complete name
image has exactly $m$ elements, provided the declared paid isolation
removes all other acquired distinctions. Use fixed-width name storage,
a public finite computation schedule and allocation, and Assumption
1.3's export and isolation to impose precisely that complete interface.
The acquired array, first-leaf index, label, computation fields and
transcript do not remain separately accessible.

After the singleton request arrives, name $i$ selects the actual word
$p_{a+i}$. By (GDB.106), its replies distinguish all descriptors in
that group:

| Descriptor in group $i$ | Reply at $p_{a+i}$ |
| --- | --- |
| $(a+i,0)$ | alpha |
| $(a+i,1)$ | beta |
| Its optional earlier descriptor | absent |
| Its optional later descriptor, or $B$ | branch |

The two optional entries are individually unique by (GDB.109).
A group cannot contain both a later descriptor and $B$, since the
later indices use $1,\ldots,2b$ and $B$ uses $2b+1$.
Thus the one reply and the retained name recover the full descriptor.
On unused name/reply pairs install any fixed output; they are not
actual executions. Formula (GDB.107) now gives the factor, and the
credited decoder gives every subsequently supplied authentic count,
without another Read. The query is chosen before that reply and any
count. No query is made on an evolved or copied source.

There are exactly $n$ preparation words, of lengths $1,\ldots,n$.
This proves their charges and the maximum late length. Grouping and
factor tables are public and effectively generated from $n,p$;
no preassigned source colors or actual-source table are supplied.
A name can use $\lceil\log_2m(n)\rceil$ fixed-width bits; unused bit
patterns do not enlarge its attained alphabet. Work, storage and the
paid interface services are separate charges. $\square$

The prefix scan is a sufficient preparation bound. The theorem asserts
no acquisition optimum, and does not replace the different passive
fixed-target acquisition optimum in AAR §13.

